  "_commit": {
    "id": "55a3bbff326c7fb248e6bdeabbf4129fe11c3533",
    "author": "Patrick Aboyoun <aboyoun.patrick@gene.com>",
    "committer": "Patrick Aboyoun <aboyoun.patrick@gene.com>",
    "message": "Return NA for the variance of a one-element margin with nothing stored\n\nrowVars, colVars, rowSds and colSds returned 0 for a margin with a\nsingle element in which no value is stored, such as a row of a matrix\nwith one column, where base R gives NA because a variance needs two\nvalues.\n\nA margin with no stored value has no row in the grouped result, so it is\npadded with the fill of the result seed. The four methods set that to\nthe typed zero, the right value for a margin of several identical\nelements but not for one.\n\n- Add .varFill(x, k), which returns the typed zero when the margin has\n  more than one element and NA otherwise.\n- Use it in the four seed methods, with k taken from the trailing\n  dimensions for the row functions and the leading dimensions for the\n  column functions, as the sums already scale their fill. This also\n  covers an array with dims greater than 1.\n\nTests compare the variances and standard deviations of one-column,\none-row and all-zero margins, with and without na.rm, and of a\n3-dimensional array with dims = 2, against matrixStats; they fail with\nthe change reverted (20 failures). A random oracle over one-row,\none-column and all-zero matrices gave 880 cases and 0 mismatches. Suite:\n4195 expectations, 0 failures at 1 and 8 threads; BiocDuckDB 2201\nexpectations, 0 failures.\n",
    "time": 1795280633
  },
